Before taking this course, I had never heard of TypeScript. Python was the first programming language I learned, and it is still the language that feels the most natural to me. I later gained experience with C and C++ in ECE 205, where I was introduced to object-oriented programming and GitHub. Because I already had some programming experience, learning TypeScript has not felt extremely difficult. It was more like getting used to something new. 

## Learning TypeScript

So far, I like TypeScript. I find a lot of the syntax intuitive, although there are still parts of the language that I am learning. I have also started to understand why TypeScript can be useful in software engineering. I liked that it can catch certain mistakes while developing a program and provide more structure to the code. I can see how this could be helpful when working on larger projects or with other developers. Even though Python still feels the most comfortable to me, my experience with TypeScript has been positive so far.

## Training Through WODs

I have also enjoyed the Workout of the Day exercises. I like that the WODs give us a problem and a target time without being overly restrictive about how we solve it. Since I tend to take my time when programming, having a target gives me something to improve on, even though I sometimes still fell outside of the standard times. It also helps me recognize what I can do quickly and what I need more practice with.

The idea of athletic software engineering makes sense to me because I feel like I am being trained through practice. As I understand more of the reasoning behind the code, I am able to make decisions faster. I am still working on my speed, but I already feel more confident than I did at the beginning. For me, becoming faster at programming is not just about writing code quickly, but understanding what I am doing well enough to make decisions faster. I like the idea of building a strong foundation and those baby steps stop feeling as intimidating. 
